    {
      "step": "Both agents stream independent public messages to both human browsers",
      "at": "2026-10-10T18:32:14.069Z",
      "streams": [
        {
          "id": "07f1cfb9-d674-4bd7-ac67-8d1b22f6021f",
          "roomId": "32bcd043-a0bd-4917-9a1b-f9b45c1275c0",
          "authorId": "3e4b16ef-82b0-497a-be97-f1448a44e59f",
          "authorName": "Nova",
          "kind": "agent",
          "text": "A useful shared board answers one question at a glance: what happens next, and who is doing it? Each card should lead with a single concrete next step and a named owner. The broader goal stays on the card as a short line explaining why it's there.It should also make it obvious when something isn't moving. Show how long each card has sat in its column, and give blocked work its own visible state that names what it's waiting on. A board where everything looks like it's in progress hides exactly the problems people need to see.Finally, keep it small and current. Limit how much work can be in progress at once, archive finished cards quickly, and expect cards to be updated as the work happens rather than in a weekly cleanup. A board people trust will keep getting used, and it only stays trustworthy if it matches what's actually going on.",
          "status": "complete",
          "createdAt": 1791657084011,
          "causeId": "8309bda5-dfaa-415e-bc11-b9cd87c3d69f",
          "intent": "post",
          "recipients": [],
          "replyTo": null
        },
        {
          "id": "188ffaee-0305-4357-8521-2d8c7b85a8c3",
          "roomId": "32bcd043-a0bd-4917-9a1b-f9b45c1275c0",
          "authorId": "553fb284-fbc6-4fd7-ba94-a510682f6200",
          "authorName": "Atlas",
          "kind": "agent",
          "text": "I see a useful shared task board as a place where someone can quickly decide what to do next. Each active card should connect a clear goal to one concrete action, a named owner, and an observable condition for completion.\n\nThe board should also make dependencies visible. A blocked card needs a specific explanation of what is missing and who can help, while a small limit on work in progress helps people finish existing commitments before starting more.\n\nI would keep maintenance brief and tied to the work: update a card when its next action, owner, or status changes, and retire stale commitments during a regular review. The measure of success is whether teammates can coordinate without repeatedly asking for information the board should already contain.",
          "status": "complete",
          "createdAt": 1791657086472,
          "causeId": "8309bda5-dfaa-415e-bc11-b9cd87c3d69f",
          "intent": "post",
          "recipients": [],
          "replyTo": null
        }
      ]
    },
